# Owner Duplicate-Detection / Identity-Key Logic

## Overview of the flow

The create pipeline is wired in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`. The duplicate-relevant steps, in order, are:
- `ensureEmailNotDisposable` → `EnsureOwnerEmailNotDisposable` (email-domain blocklist, 400)
- `ensureUniqueIdentity` → `EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique` (the single consolidated 409 duplicate check)

Note: `EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique`, `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique`, and `EnsureOwnerEmailUnique` classes exist with 409 handlers, but they are **NOT wired into the POST flow** — only `EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique` is. The identity key is also surfaced read-only via the `OwnerMapper`.

All classes referenced below are small single-responsibility classes (mostly single-method utility classes with a private constructor, or single `service`-method OfficeFloor functions).

---

## 1. Identity key / household id / hashing

### `IdentityKey` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/IdentityKey.java`
Final utility class, one method. Current key is **plaintext**, `telephone|email|householdId` — NOT a SHA-256 hex.
```java
public static String of(Owner owner) {
    String telephone = owner.getTelephone() == null ? "" : owner.getTelephone();
    String email = owner.getEmail() == null ? "" : owner.getEmail();
    return telephone + "|" + email + "|" + HouseholdId.of(owner);
}
```

### `HouseholdId` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/HouseholdId.java`
Final utility class. This is where SHA-256 is used for household identity: first 12 UPPER-hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + "|" + postcode`. Last-name normalization: trim, collapse whitespace, lowercase (`Locale.ROOT`). No soundex.
```java
public static String of(Owner owner) {
    String postcode = owner.getPostcode() == null ? "" : owner.getPostcode();
    String seed = key(owner.getLastName()) + "|" + postcode;
    try {
        byte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256")
                .digest(seed.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(12);
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02X", digest[i]));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
    catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(e);
    }
}

private static String key(String value) {
    return value == null ? "" : value.trim().replaceAll("\\s+", " ").toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
}
```

### `EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique.java`
The wired 409 check. Compares full `IdentityKey`, ignoring soft-deleted owners.
```java
public class EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)
            throws DuplicateIdentityException {
        String identityKey = IdentityKey.of(owner);
        boolean taken = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
                .filter(other -> !other.isDeleted())
                .anyMatch(other -> identityKey.equals(IdentityKey.of(other)));
        if (taken) {
            throw new DuplicateIdentityException("An owner with the same identity already exists");
        }
    }
}
```

### Other (unwired) uniqueness checks (each throws its own 409 exception)
- `EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique.java` — telephone equality; throws `DuplicateTelephoneException`.
- `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique.java` — same `HouseholdId`, bypassed by `sharesHousehold`; throws `DuplicateHouseholdException`.
- `EnsureOwnerEmailUnique.java` — email equality; throws `DuplicateEmailException`.

### `normalizedTelephone`
There is no field literally named `normalizedTelephone`. Telephone normalization to E.164 happens in `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/E164.java` (`normalize`), invoked from `BuildOwner.service` (`request.setTelephone(E164.normalize(...))`). The normalized value stored on the owner is what feeds `IdentityKey`. `TelephoneDisplay.java` formats it for display.

### Email-domain blocklist
`EnsureOwnerEmailNotDisposable` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/EnsureOwnerEmailNotDisposable.java` (400, not 409):
```java
private static final Set<String> BLOCKED = Set.of(
        "mailinator.com", "tempmail.com", "guerrillamail.com");

public void service(@Val Owner owner) throws DisposableEmailException {
    String email = owner.getEmail();
    int at = (email == null) ? -1 : email.lastIndexOf('@');
    if (at >= 0 && BLOCKED.contains(email.substring(at + 1))) {
        throw new DisposableEmailException("Email domain is not allowed");
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Soft-match logic

### `PossibleDuplicate` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/PossibleDuplicate.java`
This is the soft-match ("possible duplicate") detector — same last name + postcode but different telephone, excluding same-household and self. Returns earliest matching owner id or null.
```java
public static Integer of(Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    String postcode = owner.getPostcode();
    if (postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()) {
        return null;
    }
    String householdId = HouseholdId.of(owner);
    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(other -> other.getId() != null && !other.getId().equals(owner.getId()))
            .filter(other -> !householdId.equals(HouseholdId.of(other)))
            .filter(other -> postcode.equals(other.getPostcode()))
            .filter(other -> other.getLastName().equalsIgnoreCase(owner.getLastName()))
            .filter(other -> !owner.getTelephone().equals(other.getTelephone()))
            .map(Owner::getId)
            .min(Integer::compareTo)
            .orElse(null);
}
```
Consumed in `RespondWithOwner.service` and `RespondWithOwnerCreated.service` (both in `.../owner/`), setting DTO fields `possibleDuplicate` (boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (id).

Related household helpers using `HouseholdId`: `Household.java` (`size`), `MembershipCap.java` (`apply`), `Namesakes.java` (`countBefore`).

---

## 3. Escalation (409/400 handlers)
All in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/`. Each is a tiny exception class + a handler that builds a `ProblemDetail` via `ProblemDetails.build(...)`.
- `DuplicateIdentityException` + `DuplicateIdentityExceptionHandler` → 409 (`HttpStatus.CONFLICT`)
- `DuplicateHouseholdException` + handler → 409
- `DuplicateTelephoneException` + handler → 409
- `DuplicateEmailException` + handler → 409
- `DisposableEmailException` + handler → 400 (`HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST`)

All handlers are identical shape, e.g.:
```java
public void handle(@Parameter DuplicateIdentityException ex,
        ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>> response) {
    ProblemDetail detail = ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.CONFLICT, ex.getMessage());
    response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).body(detail));
}
```

---

## 4. Tests

Only one test touches this area: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (with base `AcceptanceBase.java` in the same dir).

Important: the test header describes an **"identity-key-v2"** target spec that does NOT match the current code:
```
identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName));
duplicate detection (409) uses it.
```
Two tests:
- `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` — asserts the returned `identityKey` matches `[0-9a-f]{64}` (a full lowercase SHA-256 hex). The current `IdentityKey.of` returns a pipe-delimited plaintext string, so this asserts a not-yet-implemented v2 shape.
- `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` — creates an owner, then a deep copy (identical telephone/email/lastName) and expects `409 Conflict`.

`AcceptanceBase` provides reusable helpers relevant here: `sha256hex(String)` (full lowercase hex SHA-256), `shaHex(String, n)` (first n UPPER-hex chars), `luhn(String)`, plus owner-builder/create helpers and `deleteOwner` (soft delete — noted that duplicate checks ignore deleted owners).

---

## 5. How soundex and SHA-256 are currently done

### Soundex
**Not implemented anywhere.** No `Soundex` class, and no `commons-codec` dependency (checked `pom.xml` and all of `src`). The only mention of "soundex" is the target-spec comment in `AcceptanceTest.java`. So implementing v2 would require writing a soundex routine or adding a library.

### SHA-256
No shared hashing utility class exists. SHA-256 is done inline via `java.security.MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256")` in two production classes:
- `HouseholdId.java` — first 12 UPPER-hex chars (loops `i < 6`, `%02X`).
- `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/.../owner/AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`) — `hash8`, first 8 UPPER-hex chars of SHA-256 over `telephone + lastName`.

Both use the pattern `MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(seed.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8))` then a `StringBuilder` with `String.format("%02X", ...)`. The test side (`AcceptanceBase.sha256hex`) uses the same approach but LOWER-case (`%02x`) and full 64 chars. There is no `MessageDigest`-based shared helper in `src/main`; each call site re-implements it.

### Luhn / check digit
`CheckDigit.java` (`.../owner/CheckDigit.java`) computes a Luhn check digit over customer-code digits (mirrors `AcceptanceBase.luhn`).

---

## Class-size note
Every class involved is small — single-responsibility. The utility classes (`IdentityKey`, `HouseholdId`, `PossibleDuplicate`, `Household`, `Namesakes`, `MembershipCap`, `CheckDigit`, `E164`, `TelephoneDisplay`, `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`) are 20–45 lines each with a private constructor and one public static method. The `Ensure*` and `RespondWith*` classes are single-`service`-method OfficeFloor functions. There is no large "owner service" god-class holding this logic; it is spread across these focused classes plus the MapStruct `OwnerMapper` interface.

